<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yale University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1701">1701</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Yale College</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ial">Yale</a> University is a private research university in New Haven, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, founded in 1701 and one of the oldest institutions of higher learning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Its archaeologists have led major excavations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, including Harvey Weiss's work from 1979 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-leilan">Tell Leilan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which recovered the royal archives of ancient Apum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yale-university.jpg" alt="Yale University" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yale University · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yale_University_Shield_1.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
